
  [1mB. Two independent loggers sharing only a written contract[0m
  [38;5;153mapp-logger.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/logger.js[39m, each self-contained and idiomatic to its runtime, sharing a documented record schema
  and a duplicated allow-list.
  [3mTrade-offs:[0m least plumbing, nothing clever. But it duplicates the allow-list, which is exactly the thing whose whole
  value is that it can't silently fall out of date. Two copies means the browser one gets a new permitted field and the
  Node one doesn't, and you find out from a log that's missing data or from one that isn't.
  [3mComplexity:[0m trivial.

  [1mC. Convert the repo to ES modules[0m
  [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m, [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, one genuinely shared module imported by both.
  [3mTrade-offs:[0m the cleanest structure with no dual-mode hack. Two real costs: it rewrites [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m
  from CommonJS for reasons unrelated to logging, and [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m is blocked by CORS over [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so opening
  [38;5;153mindex.html[39m directly stops working and you need a local server. That's a workflow change smuggled in under a logging
  ticket.
  [3mComplexity:[0m moderate, with unrelated blast radius.

  [1mMy recommendation: A.[0m It puts the redaction logic — the part where a mistake writes credentials to disk — in exactly
  one place, which is the entire point of choosing an allow-list. B is simpler today and quietly gives that back. C is
  the nicest end state but pays for it by converting the module system and breaking [38;5;153mfile://[39m loading, which isn't a trade
  a logging change should be making on your behalf.
